  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1045 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nFriday through Tuesday:\n\nThe upper low over Lower Michigan Friday morning should steadily\nexit to Lake Erie by Saturday morning. Brisk northerly winds and a\nhigh amount of cloud cover will linger through much of the day\nFriday, especially closer to Lake Michigan. Highs are expected to\nbe just below 50. With clearing skies Friday night as high\npressure briefly takes hold, and 850mb temps dropping to around\nnegative 4C, our low temps are expected to drop into the upper \n20s inland from Lake Michigan. Since we are well past our \nclimatological freeze dates, we will not be issuing any \nfrost/freeze headlines.\n\nThere is another potent mid level shortwave trough in line to\ncross the Upper Midwest on Saturday. This should dive south\nthrough IA and IL (or further southwest) and any winds or precip\nassociated with it will miss southern WI. Highs here Sat will be\naround 50 with light winds. Skies will trend toward sunny. \n\nSunday will bring a steady westerly breeze as weak high pressure\nmoves across the Plains. Highs will inch warmer into the mid 50s\nunder mostly sunny skies. \n\nA dry low pressure system will track across Ontario Monday. This\nwill bring breezy southwest winds to southern WI, but also help to\nwarm us back into the upper 50s to around 60. \n\nA mid level shortwave trough is expected to track across the Upper\nGreat Lakes sometime around Tuesday. It looks like the only impact\nthis might have on southern WI is the potential for brisk\nnortherly winds in its wake on Wednesday. Temperatures should\nremain mild (warmer than normal) for most of the week.  \n\nCronce",
